<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hypatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 355–<a href="/years/415">415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hypatia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> philosopher, mathematician and astronomer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, the daughter of the mathematician Theon. She taught philosophy to pagan and Christian pupils and advised the city's leaders. In March 415 she was murdered by a Christian mob during a conflict between the prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Cyril, and she later became a symbol of learning destroyed by intolerance.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hypatia was the daughter of Theon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, whose main achievement was a new edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a> correcting scribal errors made over nearly 700 years of copying; it became the most widely used edition for centuries. Like her father, Hypatia rejected the teachings of Iamblichus and embraced the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a>. Alexandria was regarded as second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as the philosophical capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a>, and she taught students from all over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. According to Damascius, she lectured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, and walked through the city in a tribon, a cloak associated with philosophers, giving impromptu public lectures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hypatia was probably more a teacher and commentator than an innovator. No evidence shows that she published independent philosophical works, but scholars of her time preserved and commented on classical mathematical texts rather than publishing new ones. She is now known to have edited Book III of Ptolemy's Almagest; its title in Theon's commentary describes an edition revised by his daughter Hypatia, the philosopher. Her contribution is thought to be an improved tabular method for the long divisions needed in astronomical computation. She also wrote a commentary on Diophantus's Arithmetica, which may survive in part, and one on the conic sections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-perga">Apollonius of Perga</a>, which has not survived.</p><p>A letter of her pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synesius">Synesius</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ptolemais, says she taught him to construct a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> plane <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">astrolabe</a> as a gift for an official. Ancient sources record that she was widely loved by pagans and Christians alike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Socrates Scholasticus, in March 415, during Lent, a mob of Christians led by a lector named Peter seized Hypatia's carriage as she travelled home, dragged her into the Kaisarion, a former pagan temple turned church, and killed her. Her body was torn apart and burned. Although no firm evidence linked the bishop Cyril to the murder, it was widely believed that he had ordered it, and the Alexandrian council sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; the advisers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> began an investigation. Her death shocked the empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonists</a> such as Damascius grew more fervent in their opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> for centuries afterwards. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> she was part of the basis for the legend of Saint Catherine of Alexandria. Charles Kingsley's novel Hypatia of 1853 called her the last of the Hellenes. Some modern portrayals link her death to the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>, although the library no longer existed in her lifetime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hypatia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pagan institutions faded meanwhile: the Serapeum of Alexandria was destroyed in 391, the philosopher <b>Hypatia</b> was murdered by a Christian mob in 415, and Justinian had the temple of Isis at Philae closed in the 530s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=394" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hypatia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=394">What was here in 394 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
